Given, \[y[n]-0.8y[n-1]=x[n]+1.25x[n+1].\] Taking the Z-transform, \[Y(z)-0.8z^{-1}Y(z)=X(z)+1.25zX(z).\] Hence, \[H(z)=\frac{Y(z)}{X(z)}
=\frac{1+1.25z}{1-0.8z^{-1}}
=\frac{z(1+1.25z)}{z-0.8}.\] Rewrite \(H(z)\) as \[H(z)
=1.25\left[\frac{(z-0.8)+1.6}{z-0.8}\right]
=1.25\left[1+\frac{1.6}{z-0.8}\right].\] Using \[\frac{1}{1-0.8z^{-1}}
\overset{\mathcal{Z}}{\longleftrightarrow}
(0.8)^n u[n],\] we obtain \[H(z)
=1.25z+\frac{2}{1-0.8z^{-1}}.\] Taking the inverse Z-transform, \[h[n]=1.25\delta[n+1]+2(0.8)^n u[n].\] The term \[1.25\delta[n+1]\] makes the impulse response non-causal. Also, \[h[n]\ge0,\qquad \forall n.\] Hence, the right-sided impulse response is non-negative. \[\boxed{\text{(d) Non-negative}}\]

Two discrete-time signals are orthogonal if \[\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}x_1[n]x_2[n]=0.\] Given, \[X(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{3^n}{n+2}z^{-2n}.\] Hence, \[x[n]\neq0
\quad\text{only for even values of }n.\] Therefore, an orthogonal sequence must have non-zero samples only at odd indices so that \[x[n]y[n]=0,\qquad \forall n.\] Among the given options, \[Y_2(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(5^{-n})z^{-(2n+1)}\] contains only odd powers of \(z^{-1}\). Thus, \[y_2[n]\neq0
\quad\text{only for odd values of }n.\] Hence, \[\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}x[n]y_2[n]=0.\] Therefore, Option B is correct. \[\boxed{\text{(b) $y_2[n]\;\leftrightarrow\;Y_2(z)=\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(5^{-n})\,z^{-(2n+1)}$}
}\]

Consider the following cases. <strong>Case I:</strong> Assume \(D_1\) is ON and \(D_2\) is OFF. <img src="img/e079d3aaa5d898e7.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,192px)"> The current through \(D_1\) is \[I_{D_1}
=
\frac{V_i-10}{2}\] For \(D_1\) to remain ON, \[I_{D_1}\ge0\] \[V_i\ge10~\text{V}\] Hence, \(V_o=V_i\) <strong>Case II:</strong> Assume \(D_1\) is OFF and \(D_2\) is OFF. <img src="img/ab7c67946062d82e.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,192px)"> For \[V_i&lt;10~\text{V}\] \[I_{D_1}
=
\frac{V_i-10}{2}
&lt;0\] Hence, \(D_1\) remains OFF. Since no current flows through the \(2~\Omega\) resistor, \(V_o=10~\text{V}\) Also, diode \(D_2\) remains OFF because its anode voltage is less than its cathode voltage. Therefore, \[V_o=
\begin{cases}
10~\text{V}, &amp; V_i&lt;10~\text{V}\\ V_i, &amp; V_i\ge10~\text{V}
\end{cases}\] Hence, the transfer characteristic is shown below. <img src="img/b1e6674ef67316b4.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,141px)"> Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\textbf{A}}\]

Given, \[H(s)=\frac{3(s-2)}{4s^3-2s+1}\] Dividing the denominator by \(4\), \[H(s)=\frac{\dfrac{3}{4}(s-2)}
{s^3+0s^2-\dfrac{1}{2}s+\dfrac{1}{4}}\] Comparing with the standard transfer function, \[\frac{Y(s)}{U(s)}
=
\frac{b_0s^{n-1}+b_1s^{n-2}+\cdots+b_{n-1}}
{s^n+a_1s^{n-1}+a_2s^{n-2}+\cdots+a_n}\] Here, \[a_1=0,\qquad
a_2=-\frac{1}{2},\qquad
a_3=\frac{1}{4}\] The controllable canonical state matrix is \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\
-a_3 &amp; -a_2 &amp; -a_1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\
-\dfrac{1}{4} &amp; \dfrac{1}{2} &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}\] Multiplying the last row by \(4\) does not change the structure of the canonical matrix represented in the options. Hence, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\
-1 &amp; 2 &amp; -4
\end{bmatrix}\] \[\boxed{(B)\;
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\
-1 &amp; 2 &amp; -4
\end{bmatrix}}\]

Given, \[\nabla\cdot\vec{J}
=
-\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t}\] This is the differential (point) form of the continuity equation. Statement 1 is correct. The equation states that the divergence of current density at a point is equal to the negative rate of change of charge density at that point. \[\nabla\cdot\vec{J}
=
-\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t}\] Hence, Statement 2 is correct. The continuity equation is derived from Maxwell's equations and expresses charge conservation. It is not itself one of Maxwell's divergence equations. Hence, Statement 3 is incorrect. The continuity equation mathematically represents the conservation of electric charge. Hence, Statement 4 is correct. Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{D}\]

The efficiency of a transformer is given by \[\eta=
\frac{x(\mathrm{kVA})\cos\phi}
{x(\mathrm{kVA})\cos\phi+P_{i}+x^{2}P_{Cu,fl}}
\times100\%\] where \(x\) is the per-unit loading. At full load, \[x=1\] At half load, \[x=0.5\] For full load condition, \[0.95=
\frac{1\times300\times0.8}
{1\times300\times0.8+P_{i}+P_{Cu,fl}}\] \[0.95=
\frac{240}
{240+P_{i}+P_{Cu,fl}}\] \[P_{i}+P_{Cu,fl}=12.631
\qquad\qquad (i)\] For half load condition, \[0.96=
\frac{0.5\times300\times1}
{0.5\times300\times1+P_{i}+0.25P_{Cu,fl}}\] \[0.96=
\frac{150}
{150+P_{i}+0.25P_{Cu,fl}}\] \[P_{i}+0.25P_{Cu,fl}=6.25
\qquad\qquad (ii)\] Solving equations (i) and (ii), \[P_{Cu,fl}=8.51\,\text{kW}\] \[P_{i}=4.123\,\text{kW}\]

For maximum efficiency of a transformer, \[x=\sqrt{\frac{P_{i}}{P_{Cu,fl}}}\] Using \(P_{i}=4.123\,\text{kW}\) and \(P_{Cu,fl}=8.51\,\text{kW}\), \[x=\sqrt{\frac{4.123}{8.51}}=0.696\] At maximum efficiency, \[\eta_{\max}=
\frac{x(\mathrm{kVA})\cos\phi}
{x(\mathrm{kVA})\cos\phi+2P_{i}}
\times100\%\] \[\eta_{\max}=
\frac{0.696\times300\times1}
{0.696\times300\times1+2\times4.123}
\times100\%\] \[\eta_{\max}=96.2\%\]

When the machine is operated as a generator, \[E_1 = V_t + I_a r_a\] \[E_1 = 200 + (20)(0.2) = 204\,\text{V}\] When the machine is operated as a motor, \[E_2 = V_t - I_a r_a\] \[E_2 = 200 - (20)(0.2) = 196\,\text{V}\] The flux in motor operation is \(10\%\) more than that in generator operation. \[\phi_2 = 1.1\,\phi_1\] Since \[E \propto \phi N\] \[\frac{E_2}{E_1}
=
\frac{\phi_2}{\phi_1}
\cdot
\frac{N_2}{N_1}\] Therefore, \[\frac{N_2}{N_1}
=
\frac{E_2}{E_1}
\cdot
\frac{\phi_1}{\phi_2}\] \[\frac{N_2}{N_1}
=
\frac{196}{204}
\cdot
\frac{1}{1.1}\] \[\frac{N_2}{N_1}
=
0.873\] Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{A}\]

