=\frac{120\times 50}{4}
=1500\,\text{rpm}\] Given, \[N_{r1}=1440\,\text{rpm}\] Hence, \[N_{s1}-N_{r1}
=1500-1440
=60\,\text{rpm}\] At \[f_2=30\,\text{Hz}\] \[N_{s2}=\frac{120f_2}{P}
=\frac{120\times 30}{4}
=900\,\text{rpm}\] Since rotor speed deficit remains constant, \[N_{s1}-N_{r1}
=
N_{s2}-N_{r2}\] \[60
=
900-N_{r2}\] \[N_{r2}
=
900-60
=
840\,\text{rpm}\] Therefore, \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Under constant \(\dfrac{V}{f}\) control, air-gap flux remains approximately constant.</li><li>For constant load torque, slip frequency remains nearly constant.</li><li>Hence, the speed difference \((N_s-N_r)\) remains constant.</li><li>Synchronous speed is given by \(N_s=\dfrac{120f}{P}\).</li></ul></div>

