Given, \[I_{FL}=20\,\text{A}\] \[I_{BR}=15\,\text{A}\] Copper loss obtained from blocked rotor test: \[P_{cu,BR}=762\,\text{W}\] Since copper loss varies as the square of current, \[P_{cu,FL}
=
\left(\frac{I_{FL}}{I_{BR}}\right)^2
P_{cu,BR}\] \[P_{cu,FL}
=
\left(\frac{20}{15}\right)^2
\times 762
=
1354.67\,\text{W}\] Core loss at rated voltage: \[P_{core}=1002\,\text{W}\] Hence, total losses are \[P_{loss}
=
P_{cu,FL}+P_{core}\] \[P_{loss}
=
1354.67+1002
=
2356.67\,\text{W}\] Given output power, \[P_{out}=10000\,\text{W}\] Efficiency, \[\eta
=
\frac{P_{out}}
{P_{out}+P_{loss}}
\times 100\] \[\eta
=
\frac{10000}
{10000+2356.67}
\times 100
=
80.93\%\] \[\eta \approx 81\%\] \[\boxed{B}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Copper loss varies as the square of current, i.e., \(P_{cu}\propto I^2\).</li><li>Blocked rotor test is used to determine the full-load copper loss.</li><li>Core loss is assumed constant at rated voltage.</li><li>Efficiency is given by \(\eta=\dfrac{P_{out}}{P_{out}+P_{loss}}\times100\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Using a linear current ratio instead of a squared current ratio for copper loss calculation.</li><li>Omitting core loss while calculating total losses.</li></ul></div>

