=
Z_{\text{old}}
\left(\frac{S_{\text{new}}}{S_{\text{old}}}\right)
\left(\frac{V_{\text{old}}}{V_{\text{new}}}\right)^2\] Taking generator-side base values, \[S_{\text{new}}=20\ \text{MVA}\] \[V_{\text{new}}=69\ \text{kV}\] Therefore, \[Z_{\text{new}}
=
0.72
\left(\frac{20}{10}\right)
\left(\frac{69}{13.8}\right)^2\] \[=
0.72 \times 2 \times 25\] \[=
36\ \text{pu}\] Hence, the value of the load in the generator circuit is \[\boxed{\text{(A)} \ 36\ \text{pu}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Per-unit impedance changes with change of base MVA and base voltage.</li><li>\[Z_{\text{new}}
=
Z_{\text{old}}
\left(\frac{S_{\text{new}}}{S_{\text{old}}}\right)
\left(\frac{V_{\text{old}}}{V_{\text{new}}}\right)^2\]</li><li>Always use corresponding voltage bases on the side where the impedance is referred.</li></ul></div>

